# No emojis in code, comments, or logs

## Requirement

- Do not insert emoji characters (Unicode emoji, pictographs, or emoji sequences) in:
  - Source code (any language)
  - Inline comments and block comments
  - Log messages and structured logging fields
  - String literals intended for runtime output (console, ROS logs, HTTP responses from code, etc.)

## Rationale

- Keeps diffs and log aggregation grep-friendly and encoding-safe across terminals, CI, and remote shells.
- Avoids mixed-font rendering and accessibility issues in tooling.

## Allowed

- Plain ASCII text; standard punctuation.
- If status must be visual in a UI, use words, severity levels, or existing UI components—not emoji in application code.

## Not covered

- User-facing documentation or marketing copy outside the repo is out of scope unless the user asks otherwise.
- Third-party content or data the code only displays verbatim is not something to “fix” unless editing that string.
